## Laws of photometry.

law-illuminance = Illuminance
law-illuminance-statement-novice = Illuminance is how much light falls on each square metre of a surface. It is measured in lux.
law-illuminance-statement-school = Illuminance E = Φ/S is luminous flux per unit area; 1 lx = 1 lm/m².
law-illuminance-story-school = This is the definition of a quantity.
law-luminous-intensity = Luminous intensity
law-luminous-intensity-statement-novice = Luminous intensity is how much light a source sends one way. It is measured in candelas.
law-luminous-intensity-statement-school = Luminous intensity I is luminous flux per unit solid angle; 1 lm = 1 cd·sr. A 1 cd source shining alike in all directions gives a flux of 4π lm.
law-luminous-intensity-story-school = This is the definition of a quantity.
law-inverse-square-light = The inverse-square law for light
law-inverse-square-light-statement-novice = The farther from the lamp, the less light: twice as far — four times less. A tilted surface is lit more weakly.
law-inverse-square-light-statement-school = The illuminance from a point source E = I · cos α / r²: inversely proportional to the square of the distance and proportional to the cosine of the rays’ angle of incidence.
law-inverse-square-light-story-school = In 1604, in his book Ad Vitellionem paralipomena, Johannes Kepler argued that light from a point source weakens as the inverse square of the distance. In 1760, in his Photometria, Johann Heinrich Lambert demonstrated it and established the cosine law for a tilted surface.
law-inverse-square-light-limits-school = The law is exact for a point source, and for an evenly bright sphere — from its centre. For a long lamp, or a lamp with a reflector or a shade close by, it does not hold; in fog or smoke the light is also absorbed.
law-luminous-efficacy = Luminous efficacy
law-luminous-efficacy-statement-novice = Luminous efficacy is how much light a lamp gives for each watt of power.
law-luminous-efficacy-statement-school = Luminous efficacy η = Φ / P is a lamp’s luminous flux per unit of its electric power, lm/W.
law-luminous-efficacy-story-school = This is the definition of a quantity.
